// ALG1 Unit 02 Lesson 08 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Putting It All Together
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.AR.1; MLA.A1.FND.5

::ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.1]
Solve \(3x+7=22\).
{
~A. 3#This subtracts 7 but does not divide 15 by 3 correctly.
=B. 5#Correct. Subtract 7 to get \(3x=15\), then divide by 3.
~C. 15#This is the value of \(3x\), not x.
~D. 29#This adds 7 instead of using inverse operations.
}

::ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.1]
Solve \(2(x+4)=18\).
{
~A. 9#This divides by 2 but stops before subtracting 4 from both sides.
~B. 7#This divides 18 by 2 but forgets to subtract 4.
~C. 14#This subtracts 4 from 18 and stops.
=D. 5#Correct. Divide by 2 to get \(x+4=9\), then subtract 4.
}

::ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.5]
Solve \(A=lw\) for \(w\).
{
=A. \(w=A/l\)#Correct. Divide both sides by l to isolate w.
~B. \(w=Al\)#This multiplies by l instead of dividing by l.
~C. \(w=l/A\)#This reverses the needed ratio.
~D. \(w=A-l\)#Subtraction does not undo multiplication by l.
}

::ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.1]
The equation \(5x+2=5x+9\) has what type of solution?
{
~A. One solution#Subtracting \(5x\) from both sides does not leave a true equation.
~B. Infinitely many solutions#Infinitely many solutions would leave a true statement like \(2=2\).
=C. No solution#Correct. Subtracting \(5x\) gives \(2=9\), which is false.
~D. x = 7#There is no value of x that makes \(2=9\) true.
}

::ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U02_L08_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.1]
A gym charges $20 to join plus $8 per visit. Which equation gives total cost \(C\) for \(v\) visits?
{
~A. \(C=20v+8\)#This treats 20 as the per-visit cost and 8 as the fixed fee.
=B. \(C=8v+20\)#Correct. The fixed fee is 20 and each visit adds 8.
~C. \(C=28v\)#This treats the fixed fee and visit cost as one repeated rate.
~D. \(C=20+8\)#This does not include the number of visits.
}
